</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">

<div class="report-card">
  <h1>Lichess Chess Game Analytics</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics</div>
  <div class="meta">Dataset: 19,629 unique games from Lichess.org &nbsp;|&nbsp; Analysis Date: April 2026 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Audience: Analysts</div>

  <div class="toc">
    <strong>Table of Contents</strong>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#overview">1. Dataset Overview &amp; Game Distribution</a></li>
      <li><a href="#color-advantage">2. White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</a></li>
      <li><a href="#rating-analysis">3. Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#opening-strategy">4. Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Their Success Rates</a></li>
      <li><a href="#time-control">5. Time Control Patterns and Game Characteristics</a></li>
      <li><a href="#key-findings">6. Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===========================
     SECTION 1: OVERVIEW
     =========================== -->
<div class="report-card" id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset Overview &amp; Game Distribution</h2>
  <p>
    This report analyzes <strong>{overview['total_games']:,}</strong> unique chess games from Lichess.org after removing {429:,} duplicate records.
    The dataset spans a wide rating spectrum (≈800–2700 Elo) and includes both rated and casual play under a variety of time controls.
  </p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi">
      <div class="kpi-value">{overview['total_games']:,}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Games</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi">
      <div class="kpi-value">{overview['rated_pct']}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Rated Games</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi">
      <div class="kpi-value">{stats['kpis']['avg_turns']}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Turns</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi">
      <div class="kpi-value">{stats['kpis']['checkmate_rate']}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Checkmate Rate</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi">
      <div class="kpi-value">{stats['kpis']['resign_rate']}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Resignation Rate</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi">
      <div class="kpi-value">{stats['kpis']['timeout_rate']}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Timeout Rate</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Winner Distribution</h3>
  <p>
    Across all games, white wins <strong>{overview['white_win_rate']}%</strong> of decisive games, black wins <strong>{overview['black_win_rate']}%</strong>, and <strong>{overview['draw_rate']}%</strong> end in draws.
    The raw counts are <strong>{overview['white_wins']:,}</strong> white wins, <strong>{overview['black_wins']:,}</strong> black wins, and <strong>{overview['draws']:,}</strong> draws.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['winner_pie']}" alt="Winner Distribution"></div>

  <h3>Victory Status Breakdown</h3>
  <p>
    Resignation is the dominant termination mode, accounting for <strong>{stats['kpis']['resign_rate']}%</strong> of all games.
    Checkmates occur in <strong>{stats['kpis']['checkmate_rate']}%</strong> of games, while timeouts represent <strong>{stats['kpis']['timeout_rate']}%</strong>.
    Draws (including stalemates and agreed draws) make up <strong>{overview['draw_rate']}%</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['victory_bar']}" alt="Victory Status Distribution"></div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Insight:</strong> The low draw rate ({overview['draw_rate']}%) is typical of online rapid/blitz play, where time pressure and resignation culture suppress drawn outcomes relative to classical over-the-board chess.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===========================
     SECTION 2: COLOR ADVANTAGE
     =========================== -->
<div class="report-card" id="color-advantage">
  <h2>2. White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>
  <p>
    The first-move advantage is one of the most studied phenomena in chess. In this Lichess sample, white holds a measurable but modest edge overall.
    However, the magnitude of that edge is not uniform: it shrinks at higher ratings, expands in slower time controls, and even reverses in bullet chess.
  </p>

  <h3>Overall Color Performance</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Color</th><th>Wins</th><th>Win Rate (Decisive)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>White</td><td>{overview['white_wins']:,}</td><td>{overview['white_win_rate']}%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Black</td><td>{overview['black_wins']:,}</td><td>{overview['black_win_rate']}%</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    White’s {overview['white_win_rate']}% win rate translates to an approximate <strong>+{overview['white_win_rate'] - 50:.2f} percentage-point advantage</strong> over black in decisive games.
    This is consistent with established chess theory and empirical online-chess studies.
  </p>

  <h3>Color Advantage by Rating Bracket</h3>
  <p>
    Breaking the advantage down by average player rating reveals a clear trend: the white edge is largest in the <strong>1200–1399</strong> bracket ({next(b['white_rate'] for b in stats['color_by_bracket'] if b['bracket']=='1200-1399')}%)
    and narrows to its slimmest margin at <strong>2000+</strong> ({next(b['white_rate'] for b in stats['color_by_bracket'] if b['bracket']=='2000+')}%).
    One interpretation is that stronger black players mitigate first-move disadvantage through deeper preparation and more accurate defense.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['color_bracket_bar']}" alt="Win Rate by Rating Bracket"></div>

  <h3>Color Advantage by Time Control</h3>
  <p>
    Time control dramatically reshapes the color dynamic. In <strong>bullet</strong> games, black actually outperforms white ({next(b['black_rate'] for b in stats['color_by_time'] if b['time_category']=='Bullet')}%),
    likely because white’s initiative is harder to convert with only seconds on the clock.
    The advantage peaks in <strong>classical</strong> play, where white wins {next(b['white_rate'] for b in stats['color_by_time'] if b['time_category']=='Classical')}% of decisive games—suggesting that extra thinking time allows white to press the first-move edge more effectively.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['color_time_bar']}" alt="Win Rate by Time Control"></div>

  <h3>Color Advantage by Victory Status</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Victory Status</th><th>White Win %</th><th>Black Win %</th><th>Games</th></tr>
'''

for row in stats['color_by_victory']:
    html += f"    <tr><td>{row['victory_status'].title()}</td><td>{row['white_rate']}%</td><td>{row['black_rate']}%</td><td>{row['games']:,}</td></tr>\n"

html += f'''  </table>
  <p>
    White’s edge is present across all termination types, but it is slightly larger in games ending by <strong>checkmate</strong> ({next(b['white_rate'] for b in stats['color_by_victory'] if b['victory_status']=='mate')}%) than by <strong>resignation</strong> ({next(b['white_rate'] for b in stats['color_by_victory'] if b['victory_status']=='resign')}%).
    This may reflect white’s greater ability to build attacking positions when the initiative is preserved deep into the middlegame.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight">
    <strong>Key Insight:</strong> White’s first-move advantage is real but context-dependent. It is strongest in classical time controls (+{round(next(b['white_rate'] for b in stats['color_by_time'] if b['time_category']=='Classical') - 50, 1)} pp) and weakest (reversed) in bullet (−{round(50 - next(b['white_rate'] for b in stats['color_by_time'] if b['time_category']=='Bullet'), 1)} pp). As player strength increases, the edge compresses toward parity.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===========================
     SECTION 3: RATING ANALYSIS
     =========================== -->
<div class="report-card" id="rating-analysis">
  <h2>3. Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    Elo rating is designed to predict outcomes, and the Lichess data confirms its predictive power.
    The correlation between the absolute rating differential and the higher-rated player winning is <strong>{stats['kpis']['rating_diff_correlation']}</strong>—a moderate positive relationship that strengthens dramatically at larger gaps.
  </p>

  <h3>Rating Distribution</h3>
  <p>
    Both white and black rating distributions are roughly normal, centered near 1600 Elo, with tails extending above 2700.
    The mean white rating (≈1597) is marginally higher than black (≈1589), which partially explains the aggregate white win rate.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['rating_hist']}" alt="Rating Distribution"></div>

  <h3>Win Probability by Rating Differential</h3>
  <p>
    The table below bins games by the rating difference (white − black). When white is rated 400+ points higher, white wins <strong>{next(b['white_rate'] for b in stats['rating_diff_stats'] if b['bin']=='>400')}%</strong> of decisive games.
    Conversely, when black enjoys a 400+ point edge, white wins only <strong>{next(b['white_rate'] for b in stats['rating_diff_stats'] if b['bin']=='<-400')}%</strong>.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Rating Diff (White − Black)</th><th>Games</th><th>White Win %</th><th>Higher-Rated Win %</th></tr>
'''
for row in stats['rating_diff_stats']:
    html += f"    <tr><td>{row['bin']}</td><td>{row['games']:,}</td><td>{row['white_rate']}%</td><td>{row['higher_rated_win_rate']}%</td></tr>\n"

html += f'''  </table>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['rating_diff_line']}" alt="Rating Differential vs Win Probability"></div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Analytical Note:</strong> The higher-rated player’s win rate crosses 50% at roughly a zero differential and climbs steeply beyond ±100 points.
    At gaps above 300 points, the higher-rated player wins roughly 4 out of 5 games. This non-linear relationship is why raw correlation (r = {stats['kpis']['rating_diff_correlation']}) understates the practical predictive power of rating at the extremes.
  </div>

  <h3>Game Length by Rating Bracket</h3>
  <p>
    Average game length (in half-moves) varies non-monotonically with rating.
    The longest games occur in the <strong>1400–1599</strong> bracket ({next(b['mean_turns'] for b in stats['turns_by_bracket'] if b['bracket']=='1400-1599')} turns), while the shortest are at the extremes (&lt;1200 and 2000+).
    Lower-rated players often blunder early; higher-rated players may resign prophylactically or reach theoretical draws sooner.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['turns_bracket_bar']}" alt="Game Length by Rating Bracket"></div>

  <h3>Game Length by Victory Status</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Victory Status</th><th>Mean Turns</th></tr>
'''
for status, turns in stats['kpis']['avg_turns_by_victory'].items():
    html += f"    <tr><td>{status.title()}</td><td>{turns}</td></tr>\n"

html += f'''  </table>
  <p>
    Drawn games are the longest on average ({stats['kpis']['avg_turns_by_victory']['draw']} turns), followed by timeouts ({stats['kpis']['avg_turns_by_victory']['outoftime']}).
    Resignations are shortest ({stats['kpis']['avg_turns_by_victory']['resign']} turns), reflecting early concessions when a decisive tactical blow is landed.
  </p>
</div>

<!-- ===========================
     SECTION 4: OPENING STRATEGY
     =========================== -->
<div class="report-card" id="opening-strategy">
  <h2>4. Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Their Success Rates</h2>
  <p>
    Opening choice is the first strategic decision players make. This section focuses on the top 12 ECO codes by frequency, representing the most common battlegrounds in the dataset.
  </p>

  <h3>Top Openings by Frequency</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>ECO</th><th>Opening Name</th><th>Games</th><th>White Win %</th><th>Black Win %</th></tr>
'''
for row in stats['opening_stats'][:12]:
    eco = row['eco']
    name = row['name']
    # Truncate very long names
    if len(name) > 55:
        name = name[:52] + "..."
    html += f"    <tr><td><strong>{eco}</strong></td><td>{name}</td><td>{row['games']:,}</td><td>{row['white_rate']}%</td><td>{row['black_rate']}%</td></tr>\n"

html += f'''  </table>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['openings_bar']}" alt="Top Openings Win Rate"></div>

  <h3>Openings Where Black Outperforms White</h3>
  <p>
    Among the most popular openings, black achieves a higher win rate in five codes—most notably <strong>A00</strong> (Van't Kruijs Opening, black {next(o['black_rate'] for o in stats['opening_stats'] if o['eco']=='A00')}%) and <strong>B20</strong> (Sicilian Defense: Bowdler Attack, black {next(o['black_rate'] for o in stats['opening_stats'] if o['eco']=='B20')}%).
    These lines often involve white deferring central control or playing passive setups that allow black to seize the initiative.
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>ECO</th><th>Opening Name</th><th>White Win %</th><th>Black Win %</th></tr>
'''
black_better = [o for o in stats['opening_stats'][:12] if o['black_rate'] > o['white_rate']]
for row in black_better:
    name = row['name']
    if len(name) > 55:
        name = name[:52] + "..."
    html += f"    <tr><td>{row['eco']}</td><td>{name}</td><td>{row['white_rate']}%</td><td>{row['black_rate']}%</td></tr>\n"

html += f'''  </table>

  <h3>Opening Depth and Outcomes</h3>
  <p>
    The <code>opening_ply</code> metric measures how many half-moves the opening phase lasts.
    Deeper opening lines (≥8 plies) are associated with slightly higher white win rates and lower draw rates, possibly because well-prepared white players can steer games into favorable middlegame structures.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['ply_line']}" alt="Opening Depth vs Outcomes"></div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Insight:</strong> The Nimzowitsch Defense: Kennedy Variation (<strong>B00</strong>) yields the largest white advantage ({next(o['white_rate'] for o in stats['opening_stats'] if o['eco']=='B00')}%) among top openings, while the Van't Kruijs Opening (<strong>A00</strong>) is the most punishing for white ({next(o['white_rate'] for o in stats['opening_stats'] if o['eco']=='A00')}%).
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===========================
     SECTION 5: TIME CONTROL
     =========================== -->
<div class="report-card" id="time-control">
  <h2>5. Time Control Patterns and Game Characteristics</h2>
  <p>
    Time control shapes not only how games end but also how long they last and how color advantage manifests.
    The dataset is dominated by <strong>blitz</strong> ({next(t['games'] for t in stats['time_stats'] if t['time_category']=='Blitz'):,} games, {round(next(t['games'] for t in stats['time_stats'] if t['time_category']=='Blitz')/overview['total_games']*100,1)}%), followed by <strong>rapid</strong> ({next(t['games'] for t in stats['time_stats'] if t['time_category']=='Rapid'):,} games, {round(next(t['games'] for t in stats['time_stats'] if t['time_category']=='Rapid')/overview['total_games']*100,1)}%).
  </p>

  <h3>Summary by Time Control Category</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Category</th><th>Games</th><th>Mean Turns</th><th>White Win %</th><th>Draw %</th><th>Timeout %</th></tr>
'''
for row in stats['time_stats']:
    html += f"    <tr><td>{row['time_category']}</td><td>{row['games']:,}</td><td>{row['mean_turns']}</td><td>{row['white_rate']}%</td><td>{row['draw_rate']}%</td><td>{row['timeout_rate']}%</td></tr>\n"

html += f'''  </table>

  <h3>Victory Status by Time Control</h3>
  <p>
    Classical chess sees the highest draw rate ({next(t['draw_rate'] for t in stats['time_stats'] if t['time_category']=='Classical')}%) and the lowest timeout rate ({next(t['timeout_rate'] for t in stats['time_stats'] if t['time_category']=='Classical')}%),
    while bullet chess has the highest timeout rate and almost no draws.
